From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Winter <danielwinterw@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 21:12:21 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] models: add Laya decision model support

Port ggml-org/llama.cpp#29363 (head 6367bdd2da) onto the pinned revision:
the LAYA architecture, GGUF keys and tensor names, the laya-multilingual
converter, and the self-contained tools/laya encoder + decision-head runtime
with llama-laya-cli. The upstream test fixtures are omitted.

Expose it through a narrow Skippy ABI (skippy/laya.h): open a Laya GGUF,
report its sequence budgets and special tokens, tokenize with its own
Metaspace + BPE tokenizer, and run one packed read returning raw scorer and
action-head logits per question. A GGUF declaring laya.max_len above 4,096
tokens is refused at open, and sequences are packed into passes of at most
max_len tokens, because each pass builds dense attention masks. Every exported
entry point runs inside the native exception containment.

Changes from the upstream draft:
- Compile the runtime into libllama and export its API with LLAMA_API, so
  shared builds of llama-laya-cli link.
- Let the caller name the device: none keeps the weights on the CPU as
  upstream does, "auto" takes the first GPU, and any other value selects a
  ggml backend device by name; on a GPU the CPU backend stays behind it in the
  scheduler. llama-laya-cli defaults to "auto" and takes --cpu or --device.
  Set threads through the backend registry rather than linking ggml-cpu
  directly.
- Report measured memory (skippy_laya_model_memory_v1): the weight buffer,
  the scheduler's compute buffers after one full-length warm-up read, and the
  host-side masks a full pass builds, so the host can reconcile a Laya load
  against its reservation.
- Take the act-head top-1/top-2 indices from per-question strided views of a
  descending argsort. The upstream 1-D views read the third-ranked option as
  top-2, and gave every question after the first another question's ranking.

Co-authored-by: ZhangYu <zhangyub@uniontech.com>
